1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,197
For my presentation today, I'm going to talk about the Céide Fields in the northwest of Ireland,

2
00:00:08,197 --> 00:00:12,080
one of the largest Neolithic sites in the world.

3
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:13,965
I recently visited this site

4
00:00:13,965 --> 00:00:19,620
and observed the work that is currently being done by a team of archaeologists there.

5
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:26,350
The site was first discovered in the 1930s by a local teacher, Patrick Caulfield.

6
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:30,592
He noticed that when local people were digging in the bog,

7
00:00:30,592 --> 00:00:35,220
they were constantly hitting against what seemed to be rows of stones.

8
00:00:35,220 --> 00:00:37,807
He realised that these must be walls

9
00:00:37,807 --> 00:00:42,560
and that they must be thousands of years old for them to predate the bog

10
00:00:42,560 --> 00:00:45,200
which subsequently grew over them.

11
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:50,020
He wrote to the National Museum in Dublin to ask them to investigate

12
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:52,300
but no one took him seriously.

13
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:55,383
It wasn't until 40 years later,

14
00:00:55,383 --> 00:00:57,640
when Patrick Caulfield's son Seamus

15
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:02,870
who had become an archaeologist by then, began to explore further.

16
00:01:02,870 --> 00:01:09,750
He inserted iron probes into the bog to map the formation of the stones, a traditional method

17
00:01:09,750 --> 00:01:16,630
which local people had always used for finding fuel buried in the bog for thousands of years.

18
00:01:16,630 --> 00:01:22,938
Carbon dating later proved that the site was over 5,000 years old

19
00:01:22,938 --> 00:01:26,820
and was the largest Neolithic site in Ireland.

20
00:01:26,820 --> 00:01:30,186
Thanks to the bog which covers the area,

21
00:01:30,186 --> 00:01:32,620
the remains of the settlement at Céide Fields

22
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:38,180
which is over 5,000 years old, are extremely well-preserved.

23
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:44,630
A bog is 90 percent water; its soil is so saturated that when the grasses

24
00:01:44,630 --> 00:01:46,900
and heathers that grow on its surface die

25
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:51,940
they don't fully decay but accumulate in layers.

26
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:55,008
Objects remain so well preserved in these conditions

27
00:01:55,008 --> 00:01:59,610
because of the acidity of the peat and the deficiency of oxygen.

28
00:01:59,610 --> 00:02:06,517
At least 175 days of rain a year are required for this to happen;

29
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:11,450
this part of Ireland gets an average of 225 days.

30
00:02:11,450 --> 00:02:18,040
The Neolithic farmers at Céide would have enjoyed several centuries of relative peace and stability.

31
00:02:18,040 --> 00:02:22,545
Neolithic farmers generally lived in larger communities than their predecessors,

32
00:02:22,545 --> 00:02:27,050
with a number of houses built around a community building.

33
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:29,340
As they lived in permanent settlements

34
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:33,660
Neolithic farmers were able to build bigger houses.

35
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:37,009
These weren't round as people often assume,

36
00:02:37,009 --> 00:02:42,870
but rectangular with a small hole in the roof that allowed smoke to escape.

37
00:02:42,870 --> 00:02:44,900
This is one of many innovations

38
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:51,400
and indicates that the Neolithic farmers were the first people to cook indoors.

39
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:56,460
Another new technology that Neolithic settlers brought to Ireland was pottery.

40
00:02:56,460 --> 00:03:02,050
Fragments of Neolithic pots have been found in Céide and elsewhere in Ireland.

41
00:03:02,050 --> 00:03:06,986
The pots were used for many things; as well as for storing food,

42
00:03:06,986 --> 00:03:10,120
pots were filled with a small amount of fat and

43
00:03:10,120 --> 00:03:14,200
when this was set alight, they served as lamps.

44
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:20,960
It's thought that the Céide Fields were mainly used as paddocks for animals to graze in.

45
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:28,830
Evidence from the Céide Fields suggests that each plot of land was of a suitable size to sustain an extended family.

46
00:03:28,830 --> 00:03:34,996
They may have used a system of rotational grazing in order to prevent over-grazing

47
00:03:34,996 --> 00:03:38,520
and to allow for plant recovery and regrowth.

48
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:42,669
This must have been a year-round activity as no structures have been found

49
00:03:42,669 --> 00:03:46,180
which would have been used to shelter animals in the winter.

50
00:03:46,180 --> 00:03:55,100
However, archaeologists believe that this way of life at Céide ceased abruptly. Why was this?

51
00:03:55,100 --> 00:03:59,750
Well, several factors may have contributed to the changing circumstances.

52
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:05,240
The soil would have become less productive and led to the abandonment of farming.

53
00:04:05,240 --> 00:04:13,070
The crop rotationsystem was partly responsible for this as it would have been very intensive and was not sustainable.

54
00:04:13,070 --> 00:04:15,650
But there were also climatic pressures too.

55
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:20,073
The farmers at Céide would have enjoyed a relatively dry period,

56
00:04:20,073 --> 00:04:21,680
but this began to change

57
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:26,340
and the conditions became wetter as there was a lot more rain.

58
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:33,380
It was these conditions that encouraged the bog to form over the area which survives today.

59
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:36,710
So now I'd like to show you some...
